      "original_artifact": "stage5_codegen B5_comb_output_assign",
      "problem": "The generated RTL uses registered outputs with default-zero assignments (lines 171-181) that clear every output each cycle before the state case. The reference design (ref_sd_data_master in support.sv) does NOT use defaults; instead, outputs retain their values across states unless explicitly changed. This causes systematic mismatches for all signals that should persist across state transitions (cmd_set, cmd_arg, sys_adr, tx_cycle, rx_cycle, start_tx_fifo, start_rx_fifo, a_cmp_tx, a_cmp_rx, ack_transfer, CIDAT, we_req, d_read, d_write, re_s_tx, re_s_rx, etc.). Each state must explicitly assign only the signals that change, and all others must retain their previous value.",
      "evidence": [
        "Trace shows start_tx_fifo expected=1 actual=0 at time 2370 because default clears it after GET_TX_BD sets it to 1",
        "re_s_tx mismatch at time 2310 (original) because re_s_tx not explicitly cleared in GET_TX_BD for bd_cnt>=2",
        "Multiple signals show one-cycle lag pattern (match_rate_dut_lag1 ~0.98) indicating the reference retains values while DUT clears them",
        "Reference support.sv lines 252-509 show no default assignments; each state case only sets signals that need to change"
      ],
      "override": {
        "action": "Remove default-zero assignments (lines 171-181). Ensure every state explicitly sets all signals that need to change in that state. Let all other signals retain their previous values. Match reference output logic exactly: IDLE clears all control signals; GET_TX_BD/GET_RX_BD set BD read signals and retain others; SEND_CMD clears re_s_tx/re_s_rx/start_rx_fifo and sets d_read/d_write/we_req; RECIVE_CMD clears we_req/d_read/d_write and sets start_rx_fifo; DATA_TRANSFER sets CIDAT=1 and manages transfer completion; STOP/STOP_SEND manage their signals."
      }
    },
    {
      "step": 1,
      "module": "sd_data_master",
      "correction_type": "wrong_interface",
      "original_artifact": "stage5_codegen B5_comb_output_assign",
      "problem": "The GET_RX_BD transition condition uses '>' (bd_cnt > READ_CYCLE-1) but the reference uses '>=' (bd_cnt >= READ_CYCLE-1). With READ_CYCLE=4, DUT transitions at bd_cnt>3 (bd_cnt>=4) while reference transitions at bd_cnt>=3, causing a one-cycle lag in the RX path. Additionally, the reference uses separate FSM_SEQ and output logic always blocks with NO default-zero assignments; each state only sets signals that need to change. The DUT has default-zero assignments that clear all outputs every cycle, preventing signals from persisting across states.",
      "evidence": [
        "Reference support.sv FSM_COMBO: GET_RX_BD uses bd_cnt >= (READ_CYCLE-1)",
        "DUT uses bd_cnt > (READ_CYCLE - 1) for both GET_TX_BD and GET_RX_BD",
        "Alignment diagnosis shows we_req best_alignment is dut_lag1 with match_rate 1.0",
        "Known planning correction states reference does NOT use default-zero assignments"
      ],
      "override": {
        "action": "1) Fix GET_RX_BD transition: change bd_cnt > (READ_CYCLE-1) to bd_cnt >= (READ_CYCLE-1). 2) Remove ALL default-zero assignments and instead explicitly set every signal that needs to change in each state, matching the reference output logic exactly. The IDLE state clears all signals; other states only set what they need. 3) Ensure signals that should persist (cmd_set, cmd_arg, sys_adr, tx_cycle, rx_cycle, etc.) are not cleared in states where they should hold."
      }
    }
